;; Type System Test: a guard HANDLER that touches the binding is undecidable
;; EXPECT-MODE: default
;; EXPECT-COMPILE: ok
;; EXPECT-STDERR: is not statically decidable here
;; EXPECT-STDERR: a guard handler that may run after a partial body
;; EXPECT-NO-STDERR: was not consumed
;; EXPECT-NO-STDERR: was consumed more than once

;; An exception can be raised at ANY point in the body, including after the body
;; has already consumed the qubit. So the handler's use of `q` is a second use
;; on some paths and the first use on others, and neither answer is knowable
;; here. Charging body-plus-handler would reject this program; charging the max
;; would miss the double consumption. The analysis refuses to rule and SAYS so,
;; which is the same discipline the rest of the boundary is held to.
;;
;; Compare qubit_linear_guard_body_ok_test.esk, where the handler names nothing
;; linear and the body alone gives the verdict.
(define (h (q : Qubit)) : Qubit q)
(define (z (q : Qubit)) : Qubit q)

(define (guard-touches (q : Qubit))
  (guard (e (#t (z q)))
    (h q)))
(display "a handler that touches the qubit is not decidable")
(newline)
